#
# prima-materia — the searches of the run on Isaac of Nineveh, 2026-10-20
#
# In a file of their own for the mechanical reason recorded at
# examinations/searches-jain.ttl and in prima-materia#703.
#

pmp:SyriacSignsSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Every sign of success and of failure in five discourses of Isaac of Nineveh's First Part"@en, "Jedes Gelingens- und Misslingenszeichen in fünf Reden des Ersten Teils Isaaks von Ninive"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Isaac of Nineveh, First Part: discourses XIX, XXXV, XXXVI and LXX read complete in A. J. Wensinck's translation and LX to its p. 284, and the passages entered checked in the translation of the Holy Transfiguration Monastery (rev. 2nd ed. 2020), Homilies 37, 38, 39, 60 and 68, and in Bedjan's Syriac at the places named at pmw:IsaacNinevehFirstPart; the discourses were chosen by a search of the whole of Wensinck's translation for the terms below."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any sign by which the practitioner or another is told that a practice has worked or has not, any condition under which the text says it does not work, and any account of why it failed."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In Wensinck's English over the whole translation: sign that, signs by which, this is the sign, token, miracle, wonders, discern, tears, wild beasts, forty days, three days, seven days; in the Syriac over the whole of Bedjan with the vowel points stripped: ܕܡܥܐ and ܕܡܥܬܐ (dmeʿē, dmeʿtā, tears), ܐܬܘܬܐ (ʾātwātā, signs), ܕܢܘܗܪܐ and ܡܠܐܟ (of light, angel), ܚܙܘܢܐ (ḥezwānā, vision), ܓܠܝܢܐ (gelyānā, revelation), ܨܘܡܐ (ṣawmā, fasting), ܡܝܬܐ (mitē, the dead), ܬܕܡܪܬܐ (tedmurtā, wonder); in the Holy Transfiguration English at the places entered: sign, tears, angel of light, without necessity, raise the dead."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:SyriacMawhabtaDadmaa, pmc:SyriacTartenTaksaDadmaa, pmc:SyriacDakyutaDalba, pmc:SyriacHubbaGmira, pmc:SyriacBuyaaMenBnaynasha, pmc:SyriacRpyutaBShuraya, pmc:SyriacShbiqutaMenAlaha, pmc:SyriacAmlaSriqa, pmc:SyriacShelyaBeforeShayna ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-20" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-20" ;
    skos:note "Two counts, kept apart as SPEC §15 asks. Signs of success: five — the gift of tears, the second order of tears with the changed face, seeing every man as good, the heart hot with love with tears and speech in sleep, and the hidden joy of LXX. Conditions of failure: five — bodily labour without solitude, consolation taken from men, a cold beginning or contempt of fasting, the withdrawal of help on relaxation, doubt, love of ease or presumption with its felt sign, and the absence of the inner states that makes the labour vain. Of the ten only the changed face could be seen by a second person, and only the consolation from men names an outward condition a second person could check."@en .

pmp:SyriacTermSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the five discourses name a term after which absence counts against the practice"@en, "Ob die fünf Reden eine Frist nennen, nach der das Ausbleiben gegen die Übung zählt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same five discourses in the same three witnesses."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A number of days, months or years, or a number of attempts, after which the states or signs are to be expected, or after which their absence is a verdict, or an instruction to stop."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In Wensinck's English: day, days, year, years, month, months, long time, soon, quickly, delay, cease, give up; in the Syriac only at the one place the English search raised, LXX p. 488: ܙܒܢܐ (zabnā, time), ܡܬܬܗܝܢ (mettawhīn, tarry); in the Holy Transfiguration English at the same place: long."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-20" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-20" ;
    skos:note "No term. The nearest is LXX, Bedjan p. 488: the vigil does not tarry in giving its goods, which one translator renders as will not be long and the other as will not fail one day; neither gives a number, and the sentence after it says only that by the works of inward excellence we shall soon find a partial apperception of these states. The text nowhere tells the practitioner to stop. The numbers that occur are of other kinds: one in ten thousand found worthy of spiritual prayer, a saint four days in ecstasy, the forty days of Christ's and Elijah's fast as precedent."@en .
